node_modules/
dist/
# tsc -b 的增量信息，机器本地的
*.tsbuildinfo
out/
build/
# …except the desktop app's build resources, which are sources, not output: the icon, the tray
# icons and the entitlements. They were ignored by a rule meant for compiled output, so they
# existed only on the machine that made them — every CI build shipped Electron's default icon and
# nobody could see why, because locally it always looked right.
!packages/desktop/build/
.expo/
.turbo/
*.log
.DS_Store
.env
.env.local
release/
packages/desktop/out/
packages/core/dist/

# Wrangler's local state: the simulated D1 database, R2 objects and KV entries `wrangler dev`
# writes. Machine-local scratch, and large.
.wrangler/
# Secrets for local development — the GitHub client secret, the session signing key, a GitHub
# token. The deployed Worker gets these from `wrangler secret put`; nothing goes in the repo.
.dev.vars
.dev.vars.*

# The macOS signing identity, written by `packages/desktop/scripts/make-signing-cert.sh`.
#
# This is the private key every release is signed with, and — because the certificate's fingerprint
# is what macOS uses to recognise the app across updates — it is also what every granted permission
# is tied to. It belongs in a password manager and in GitHub Secrets, nowhere else.
signing/
*.p12

# Working notes, checklists and internal design docs stay local; three directories do not.
#
# The split is by audience. `plan/` and `notes/` are written for us and for the agent working in
# this repository — half-finished thinking, and worth nothing to a reader from outside.
# `architecture/`, `adr/` and `guide/` are written for whoever opens the repository next, which
# includes us in six months. README already links to two of them.
docs/*
!docs/architecture/
!docs/adr/
!docs/guide/

# 本机的工作约定，不进仓库
AGENTS.local.md

# Git worktrees an agent checked out to work in parallel. They are whole copies of this
# repository, so leaving them untracked means the sidebar's Git panel reports "1 change" on a
# branch where everything is committed — and every tool that walks the tree reads the copy as a
# second set of sources.
.claude/worktrees/
